this is Peter Brown standing in for  freelance writer Cassidy Mosier for her  review of Ratchet and Clank for  PlayStation 4 the new Ratchet and Clank  is proof that there's still a lot of  mileage left in the 3d platformer genre  it's less of a reboot and more of a  return to form revitalizing and updating  the franchise while simultaneously  keeping its use of inventive weapons  alien worlds and quirky attitude intact  resurrecting and older franchise can be  a gamble but here the payoff is  plentiful Ratchet and Clank is a  technically rejuvenated version of the  PlayStation 2 classic it returns to the  same alien universe taking both ratchet  and his robot friend Clank to multiple  planets during their adventure  true to the series history there's a  large arsenal of weapons to play with  many of which have multiple applications  in combat pixel Iser is a powerful  blaster converting enemies into their  8-bit forms causing them to burst into a  mess of colorful squares when defeated  the bomb glow returns as a helpful  grenade tossing weapon capable of  devastating large groups of enemies then  the groovey Tron kicks out a funky beat  and distracts enemies from attacking by  launching them into a dancing frenzy  each weapon is distinct and all of them  have robust upgrade trees to make them  all the more effective against many  different enemy types there's a generous  offering of gadgets as well including a  modification that turns Clank into a jet  pack and the trespasser device that  grants ratchet access to new areas  through a hacking minigame items are  arguably more important than the weapons  since a majority of each level is built  to utilize different combinations of  them in an effort to streamline this  process necessary items are  automatically equipped to ratchet by  interaction points in each level  allowing you to focus on building up  your weapon upgrades and tinkering with  guns that best suit your playstyle as a  bonus there are benefits to backtrack  into older levels and using newfound  items to gain access to previously  unavailable areas although it maintains  the same tight and restricted  environments of its predecessors there's  a great deal of verticality hidden  elements and side objectives in each  level to lend players more content to  dig through a levels objective flow  sticks to the traditional format of  visiting planets defeating enemies and  moving on to save another planet in  peril  while the objectives rarely change  however the way things are accomplished  do one level may be a dogfight in the  sky which ratchet has to take down enemy  warships another may be a smaller more  puzzle focused challenge for Clank to  resolve but not as prevalent as the  standard shooter platformer levels but  they do serve as a nice break up  challenge in-between Ratchet and Clank  is delightfully self-aware and the story  begins with an imprisoned captain Qwark  the egomaniac leader of the Galactic  Rangers with whom ratchet has a somewhat  colorful history  throughout the game quark narrates a  story while telling a fellow inmate  about how we first met ratchet  he's endearing for a narcissist buffoon  and his narration is central to ratchet  and clanks charm he sometimes unreliable  embellishing parts of the story to make  it seem more grandiose and heroic his  enemies vanquished ratchet thorns to  head his destination destiny other times  he comments on the players in-game  actions with ratchet making smaller  marks about ratchets lack of gadgets or  movement which is as entertaining as it  is helpful strength of each character is  important because the story itself is  probably Ratchet and Clank weakest point  it's a basic story about two hapless  misfits who set out to achieve a dream  and save the galaxy from catastrophe at  the hands of a greedy and mad antagonist  other than a rush to an awkward ending  there's nothing unforgivable about it it  just serves more as a vehicle for the  characters who outshine everything else  in the narrative you alright pal Ratchet  and Clank finest attribute is its  visuals nearly everything is absolutely  stunning from the environmental design  to the character animations each planet  visited in-game looks different from any  other with brilliant color and texture  popping on-screen characters distinct  designs are borrowed from aspects of  their personalities from the goofy soul  patch and sunglasses of skid Mick Marx  to the sickly pallor of the evil  chairman drek the animations are  beautiful lending characters and  elasticity in their movement making them  appear both cartoony and fluid this  along with detailed particle effects and  vibrant environments make the game  visually spectacular Ratchet &amp; Clank is  every bit as dynamic and fun as the 2002  original it's endearing and entertaining  a veritable power fantasy fueled by  rockets and carried by specialty gadgets  ring introducing a classic franchise to  a new generation is a difficult task but  Ratchet &amp; Clank is a shining example of  how it can be done thanks for your help  no problem  see you in the next reboot  you
 

